===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The acquisition of [[title]] to, or property in, anything for a [[price]]; buying for money or its equivalent.
#: {{syn|en|emption|q1=law}}
#: {{ux|en|They offer a free hamburger with the '''purchase''' of a drink.}}
# {{senseid|en|bought item}} That which is obtained for a price in money or its equivalent.
#: {{ux|en|He was pleased with his latest '''purchase'''.}}
#* {{RQ:EarthBound|I really don’t think you can carry any more '''purchases'''.<br>You can come again after you sell something,<br>or you can simply discard an item to lighten your load.<br>Or, you might want to sell the things you don’t need here!}}
# That which is obtained, got or acquired, in any manner, honestly or dishonestly; property; possession; acquisition. 
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The act or process of [[seek]]ing and [[obtain]]ing something (e.g. [[property]], etc.)
#* {{RQ:Beaumont Fletcher Comedies and Tragedies|Bonduca|5|3|passage=I'll {{...}} get meat to save thee, / Or lose my life i’ th’ '''purchase'''.}}
# A price paid for a [[house]] or [[estate]], etc. equal to the amount of the [[rent]] or [[income]] during the stated number of years.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1848|title=The Sessional Papers printed by order of the House of Lords
|passage=Suppose a freehold house to be worth 20 years’ '''purchase''' {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable|also|figuratively}} Any [[mechanical]] [[hold]] or [[advantage]], applied to the raising or removing of heavy bodies, as by a [[lever]], a [[tackle]] or [[capstan]].
#: {{syn|en|contact|grip|hold}}
#: {{ux|en|It is hard to get '''purchase''' on a nail without a pry bar or hammer.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Capitalist Realism: Is There No Alternative?| year=2009| author=w:Mark Fisher| isbn=9781846943171| chapter=8| pages=66–67| publisher=Zero Books| passage=The problem is that the model of individual responsibility assumed by most versions of ethics have little '''purchase''' on the behavior of Capital or corporations.}}
# {{anchor|tackle_sense_anchor}}The [[apparatus]], tackle or [[device]] by which such mechanical advantage is gained and {{n-g|(in nautical terminology)}} the ratio of such a device, like a pulley, or block and tackle.
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1952|month=July|author=W. R. Watson|title=Sankey Viaduct and Embankment|journal=Railway Magazine|page=487|text=He describes the operation thus: "The heavy ram employed to impart the finishing strokes, hoisted up with double '''purchase''' and snail's pace to the summit of the Piling Engine, and then falling down like a thunderbolt on the head of the devoted timber, driving it perhaps a single half inch in to the stratum below, is well calculated to put to the test the virtue of patience, while it illustrates the old adage of—slow and sure."}}
# {{lb|en|rock climbing|uncountable}} The amount of hold one has from an individual [[foothold]] or [[ledge]].<!-- not only in rock climbing; used mostly with verbs like lose ~, find ~, give ~, get ~ -->
#: {{syn|en|foothold|support}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=2015|issue=2|author=w:Hao Jingfang| translator=w:Ken Liu| title=Folding Beijing| work=Uncanny Magazine| url=http://uncannymagazine.com/article/folding-beijing-2/| passage=At first, he was climbing down, testing for '''purchase''' with his feet. But soon, as the entire section of ground rotated, he was lifted into the air, and up and down flipped around.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260823014059/https://www.uncannymagazine.com/article/folding-beijing-2/|archivedate=23 August 2026}}
# {{lb|en|legal|dated}} Acquisition of lands or [[tenements]] by means other than [[descent]] or [[inheritance]], namely, by one's own act or agreement.
#* {{RQ:Blackstone Commentaries|passage=The difference {{...}}between the acquisition of an estate by descent and by '''purchase'''}}

