===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[buy]], obtain by [[payment]] of a price in money or its equivalent.
#: {{ux|en|to '''purchase''' land'', ''to '''purchase''' a house}}
# To [[pursue]] and [[obtain]]; to [[acquire]] by [[seeking]]; to [[gain]], obtain, or acquire.
#* {{RQ:Spenser Shepheardes Calender|May|year=1586|passage=that loves the thing he cannot '''purchase'''}}
#* {{RQ:Heywood Fair Maid|page=1|passage=They {{quote-gloss|the English}} are all on fire / To '''purchaſe''' from the Spaniard. If their Carracks / Come deeply laden, vvee ſhall tugge vvith them / For golden ſpoile.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare As You Like It|III|ii|passage=Your accent is something finer than you could '''purchase''' in so removed a dwelling.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|I|iv|passage=His faults {{...}} hereditary / Rather than '''purchased'''.}}
# To obtain by any outlay, as of [[labor]], [[danger]], or [[sacrifice]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|to '''purchase''' favor with flattery}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Lucrece|passage=One poor retiring minute {{...}} / Would '''purchase''' thee a thousand thousand friends.}}
# To [[expiate]] by a [[fine]] or [[forfeit]].
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Romeo and Juliet|III|i|passage=Not tears nor prayers shall '''purchase''' out abuses.}}
# To apply to (anything) a device for obtaining a mechanical advantage; to get a purchase upon, or apply a purchase to; to raise or move by mechanical means.
#: {{ux|en|to '''purchase''' a cannon}}
# To put forth effort to obtain anything; to [[strive]]; to exert oneself.
#* '''1523–1525''', {{w|John Bourchier, 2nd Baron Berners}}, ''Froissart's Chronicles''
#*: Duke John of Brabant '''purchased''' greatly that the Earl of Flanders should have his daughter in marriage.
# To constitute the [[buying power]] for a purchase, have a trading value.
#: {{ux|en|Many aristocratic refugees' portable treasures '''purchased''' their safe passage and comfortable exile during the revolution.}}

